1
00:00:03,860 --> 00:00:06,540
Building on our
previous discussions

2
00:00:06,540 --> 00:00:07,710
about the command line,

3
00:00:07,710 --> 00:00:09,480
in this video, we're going

4
00:00:09,480 --> 00:00:11,810
to talk in detail
about scripting.

5
00:00:11,810 --> 00:00:13,080
We're going to talk about how to

6
00:00:13,080 --> 00:00:14,505
use variables in scripts,

7
00:00:14,505 --> 00:00:15,644
how to use arguments,

8
00:00:15,644 --> 00:00:18,780
and how to do conditional
handling in scripting.

9
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:21,120
Scripting is great
because it allows us

10
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,180
to automate frequently
used commands.

11
00:00:23,180 --> 00:00:26,340
To avoid having to retype
things on the command line,

12
00:00:26,340 --> 00:00:27,840
or maybe even retype things with

13
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:29,935
slight changes depending on

14
00:00:29,935 --> 00:00:32,470
specific things you're
doing on the command line

15
00:00:32,470 --> 00:00:34,040
that might be a slight variation

16
00:00:34,040 --> 00:00:35,660
of what you do in other cases,

17
00:00:35,660 --> 00:00:38,329
scripting allows us to record

18
00:00:38,329 --> 00:00:41,300
a common series of operations
and then fill in maybe

19
00:00:41,300 --> 00:00:42,530
some unique parts based on

20
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:44,300
variables that
were customized in

21
00:00:44,300 --> 00:00:45,660
the script either via

22
00:00:45,660 --> 00:00:47,000
command-line arguments or via

23
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:49,410
variables we said in the script.

24
00:00:49,720 --> 00:00:52,955
I like to think about
scripts as documentation.

25
00:00:52,955 --> 00:00:55,430
If you find yourself
writing a bunch of

26
00:00:55,430 --> 00:00:59,075
commands in a series in
order to perform some task,

27
00:00:59,075 --> 00:01:00,485
you should be thinking about

28
00:01:00,485 --> 00:01:02,210
turning that into
a script instead.

29
00:01:02,210 --> 00:01:04,670
Because rather than
telling someone, "Hey,

30
00:01:04,670 --> 00:01:06,050
run these five commands in a

31
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:07,790
row," you could just say, "Hey,

32
00:01:07,790 --> 00:01:09,800
run the script I
created that contains

33
00:01:09,800 --> 00:01:12,745
the commands that you need
in order to do this task."

34
00:01:12,745 --> 00:01:17,375
Often the script files are
in files ending with.sh,

35
00:01:17,375 --> 00:01:19,805
where.sh is short for shell,

36
00:01:19,805 --> 00:01:22,715
just to show that these
files are script files.

37
00:01:22,715 --> 00:01:24,170
That's convention, but it

38
00:01:24,170 --> 00:01:26,670
doesn't necessarily
need to be the case.

39
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:32,090
The one thing you'll notice
about scripts is that a lot

40
00:01:32,090 --> 00:01:33,650
of them start with
this syntax at

41
00:01:33,650 --> 00:01:36,100
the top with what's
called a shebang,

42
00:01:36,100 --> 00:01:38,405
with a number sign,

43
00:01:38,405 --> 00:01:41,495
the bang symbol,
and then /bin/sh.

44
00:01:41,495 --> 00:01:43,910
What this is doing is telling

45
00:01:43,910 --> 00:01:46,100
the shell which
shell interpreter

46
00:01:46,100 --> 00:01:48,965
to use when it executes
the script file.

47
00:01:48,965 --> 00:01:51,530
/bin/sh is a path
that represents

48
00:01:51,530 --> 00:01:53,975
where you could find in
the root file system,

49
00:01:53,975 --> 00:01:57,740
a reference to the Bourne shell

50
00:01:57,740 --> 00:02:01,325
or the equivalent of the
Bourne shell interpreter.

51
00:02:01,325 --> 00:02:06,690
This is known as the shebang
portion of the script.

52
00:02:06,690 --> 00:02:09,470
It's also common
in this location

53
00:02:09,470 --> 00:02:13,520
to see a number!/bin/bash.

54
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:15,020
What this means is that

55
00:02:15,020 --> 00:02:22,580
the Bourne shell that

56
00:02:22,580 --> 00:02:23,915
we talked about earlier

57
00:02:23,915 --> 00:02:28,020
is available to this
particular script.

58
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:31,505
As an example of

59
00:02:31,505 --> 00:02:34,140
a more complicated
script than the one

60
00:02:34,140 --> 00:02:36,780
I showed earlier
with helloworld.sh,

61
00:02:36,780 --> 00:02:38,420
here we've got a variable

62
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:40,160
that we can use to
set the greeting.

63
00:02:40,160 --> 00:02:43,115
By default, the script
will tell us hello world,

64
00:02:43,115 --> 00:02:45,530
and we have the option to
customize the greeting

65
00:02:45,530 --> 00:02:48,370
here by changing the
value of the variable.

66
00:02:48,370 --> 00:02:51,890
That's a simple example of
using a script variable

67
00:02:51,890 --> 00:02:53,090
as a variable that we can define

68
00:02:53,090 --> 00:02:55,070
ourselves as shown in
the previous slide,

69
00:02:55,070 --> 00:02:56,675
where we can customize
the greeting.

70
00:02:56,675 --> 00:02:58,340
Another thing that we
can do with variables

71
00:02:58,340 --> 00:02:59,660
is we can set them based

72
00:02:59,660 --> 00:03:03,850
on values that are passed
into the script as arguments.

73
00:03:03,850 --> 00:03:07,040
If you use the $1 syntax,

74
00:03:07,040 --> 00:03:08,180
what this is saying is the first

75
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:09,860
argument that's passed into

76
00:03:09,860 --> 00:03:12,645
the helloworldargs shell script

77
00:03:12,645 --> 00:03:14,505
will be set as the greeting.

78
00:03:14,505 --> 00:03:17,640
You can see if I do
helloworldargs.sh hola,

79
00:03:17,640 --> 00:03:20,415
then I get hola world
instead of hello world.

80
00:03:20,415 --> 00:03:22,940
Those are a couple options
that you have to customize

81
00:03:22,940 --> 00:03:26,615
the flow of the script
based on variables.

82
00:03:26,615 --> 00:03:29,210
There are several special
built-in variables

83
00:03:29,210 --> 00:03:30,590
for scripts that you can

84
00:03:30,590 --> 00:03:35,700
use to customize your
processing of the script.

85
00:03:35,700 --> 00:03:38,840
$0 is going to be the invoked
name of the bash script.

86
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:40,520
That includes the path that's

87
00:03:40,520 --> 00:03:43,180
specified to run the script.

88
00:03:43,180 --> 00:03:47,165
Then $1-$9 are going to be
the first nine arguments.

89
00:03:47,165 --> 00:03:48,830
$ number gives you the number of

90
00:03:48,830 --> 00:03:50,330
arguments that were
passed to the script.

91
00:03:50,330 --> 00:03:52,070
If you want to check
for how many arguments

92
00:03:52,070 --> 00:03:53,845
were specified,
you can use that.

93
00:03:53,845 --> 00:03:55,700
Then $? is going to give you

94
00:03:55,700 --> 00:03:57,850
the exit status of the
most recent process.

95
00:03:57,850 --> 00:03:59,870
This one's useful
for figuring out,

96
00:03:59,870 --> 00:04:01,655
did the last command
that I issued

97
00:04:01,655 --> 00:04:04,770
in the script succeed or fail?

98
00:04:05,180 --> 00:04:08,105
In addition to using
variables to control

99
00:04:08,105 --> 00:04:10,415
flow of the script,

100
00:04:10,415 --> 00:04:12,350
there's also
conditional syntax that

101
00:04:12,350 --> 00:04:14,540
lets you pick sections
of the script to

102
00:04:14,540 --> 00:04:17,330
execute based on things like

103
00:04:17,330 --> 00:04:21,290
location of files or
values in variables.

104
00:04:21,290 --> 00:04:23,480
What you see with this often,

105
00:04:23,480 --> 00:04:26,090
is the bracket syntax here,

106
00:04:26,090 --> 00:04:28,490
where bracket is
really a shorthand

107
00:04:28,490 --> 00:04:31,000
for use of the test command.

108
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:32,420
If you do a man test,

109
00:04:32,420 --> 00:04:34,580
you can see all the options
for the test that you can

110
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:37,145
perform as part of
a bash conditional.

111
00:04:37,145 --> 00:04:40,025
In this case, what I'm
checking to do is to see if

112
00:04:40,025 --> 00:04:43,025
the writeDir variable here

113
00:04:43,025 --> 00:04:46,545
is a directory in
the file system.

114
00:04:46,545 --> 00:04:48,830
You can see this if you
look at the man page

115
00:04:48,830 --> 00:04:51,605
for test and see what
the -d option is.

116
00:04:51,605 --> 00:04:54,725
This says if I use -d file,

117
00:04:54,725 --> 00:04:58,805
the test command's
going to return

118
00:04:58,805 --> 00:05:02,585
a match success status

119
00:05:02,585 --> 00:05:05,750
if the file exists
and is a directory.

120
00:05:05,750 --> 00:05:10,460
If -d writeDir is going to
execute this part of the

121
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:15,420
conditional if writeDir
is a directory,

122
00:05:15,420 --> 00:05:16,760
and it's going to
execute this part of the

123
00:05:16,760 --> 00:05:18,710
conditional if it
was not a directory.

124
00:05:18,710 --> 00:05:19,730
You can see, in

125
00:05:19,730 --> 00:05:21,050
this case, we're going
to exit with one,

126
00:05:21,050 --> 00:05:22,475
which is going to
be an error status

127
00:05:22,475 --> 00:05:25,590
if the directory wasn't created.

128
00:05:27,020 --> 00:05:30,770
The exit 1 is an
example of how you can

129
00:05:30,770 --> 00:05:32,765
communicate success and failure

130
00:05:32,765 --> 00:05:35,970
through the exit
status of your script.

131
00:05:36,040 --> 00:05:39,110
Exit 0 is traditionally
associated

132
00:05:39,110 --> 00:05:41,910
with a success response,

133
00:05:41,910 --> 00:05:43,520
and exit 1 is traditionally

134
00:05:43,520 --> 00:05:45,980
associated with some
problem that occurred,

135
00:05:45,980 --> 00:05:47,675
or really any non-zero exit

136
00:05:47,675 --> 00:05:50,245
is associated with some
problem that occurred.

137
00:05:50,245 --> 00:05:54,110
Then one thing to be
aware of is you can use

138
00:05:54,110 --> 00:05:56,240
the output of your script to

139
00:05:56,240 --> 00:05:58,660
help direct the user
how to actually use it.

140
00:05:58,660 --> 00:06:01,310
In this case, this
is showing one

141
00:06:01,310 --> 00:06:04,465
of the scripts that you'll
write for assignment number 1.

142
00:06:04,465 --> 00:06:06,290
When you write it
with no arguments,

143
00:06:06,290 --> 00:06:08,435
it'll tell you that it
needs some arguments,

144
00:06:08,435 --> 00:06:09,980
tells you which
arguments it needs,

145
00:06:09,980 --> 00:06:11,900
and a little bit more

146
00:06:11,900 --> 00:06:14,910
about what you should
specify in the arguments.